  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nSaturday morning, a weak 500-mb trough looks to be over the forecast \nregion. Surface winds out of the northwest are possible with a weak \ncold front across Eastern Colorado. However, high temperatures are \nforecast to rise into the 80s across the forecast area despite the \ntroughing overhead and weak cold front. This would likely be due to \nweaker surface winds alongside clearing of cloud cover, which \nwould allow solar heating to dominate as the primary heating \nmechanism for the day. This solution is supported by LREF \nguidance, which shows a 60% chance or higher for less than 10% \ncloud cover along and west of U.S. Highway 83. In addition, NBM\nguidance suggests a 50% chance or greater that surface winds \nwill remain below 10 mph across most of the forecast region. \nEven NBM 25th percentile high temperatures are in the 80s across\nthe CWA (County Warning Area) except along U.S. Highway 283 \nwhere lingering cloud cover may keep highs in the upper 70s. It\nshould be noted that forecast guidance indicates relative \nhumidities dropping into the teens across portions of Eastern \nColorado Saturday afternoon, which could indicate some potential\nfor critical fire weather conditions. However, NBM guidance \nsuggests a 1 in 3 chance or less that wind gusts will exceed 25 \nmph across most of this area, which leaves confidence in fire \nweather conditions occurring less than 5%. \n\nRidging looks to build into the forecast area Sunday, with ensemble \nguidance suggesting that this will transition into more \nsouthwesterly upper-level flow by Monday morning. Conditions \ncontinue to be forecast in the upper 70s to mid 80s across the \nforecast region. Precipitation remains unlikely at least through the \nmorning hours on Monday, as NBM precipitation guidance remains below \na 10% probability for all areas that greater than 0.1 inches of rain \nwill occur. \n\nTroughing is forecast to begin moving overhead sometime Monday \nafternoon or evening. A surface low at the leading edge of this \ntrough would be expected to deepen with the aid of sufficient upper-\nlevel divergence and lee cyclogenesis across the Central and North-\nCentral Plains. A cold front appears likely as the surface low \npasses through the forecast area sometime between the afternoon and \nnight hours Monday. Chances for precipitation increase dramatically \nalong the frontal boundary, as NBM guidance shows that most areas \nhave at least a 40% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of rain with \nthis activity through 6 am Tuesday. Areas north and east of a line \nfrom Trenton, NE to Hill City, KS have as high as a 75% chance for \nthe same. Some of this activity may be associated with \nthunderstorms, particularly along and east of U.S. Highway 83 where \nLREF guidance shows between a 1 in 2 and 1 in 3 chance that surface \nCAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) will be above 1000 J/kg. \nThe same guidance suggests between a 40 and 50% chance that sfc-\n500mb wind shear will exceed 30 knots, which could allow some \nthunderstorms to become severe if they develop. High winds would be \nthe biggest concern with these storms, as GFS and EC model soundings \nsuggest the vertical wind shear would be along the cold front. \nAdditionally, CAPE and wind shear seem like they would be a bit low \nfor large hail and tornadoes to occur with storms. As such, a \nreasonable forecast would be widespread showers and thunderstorms \nalong the cold front, with perhaps a storm or two having a chance to \nproduce severe wind. Confidence in severe weather occurring Monday \nnight and into Tuesday is around 5%. \n\nForecast guidance becomes a lot more uncertain by Tuesday morning. \nEnsemble guidance is in support of troughing remaining overhead into \nthe evening hours, but becoming less certain on how quickly the \nupper trough will move out of the forecast area. LREF guidance \ndoesn't seem to favor the trough moving out of the area until \nWednesday morning, when about 55-60% of the members show 500-mb \nheights above 576 dam (5760 meters). However, even by Wednesday \nafternoon, 30% of the members still show the trough in the area. \nCooler temperatures seem likely Tuesday afternoon, with highs \nforecast in the upper 60s to mid 70s. However, conditions on \nWednesday would depend on whether the trough is still in the area or \nnot. If the feature has moved off to the east, warmer and drier \nconditions could be expected, with highs in the mid 70s to 80s. \nOtherwise, cooler and wetter conditions would be expected to remain \nin place.",
